# Commander keyboard - the microcontroller circuit, soldered straight onto the keyboard PCB in place of the Arduino
# Micro module (U1 of the Altium design).
#
# Since 2026-11-19 (user's request: QMK / VIA): a Raspberry Pi RP2040 (LCSC C2040) with its QSPI flash, 12 MHz crystal
# and a 3.3 V LDO, instead of the STC8H8K64U of 2026-11-15 (which needed a firmware of its own).  QMK runs on the
# RP2040 as it is, VIA with it; firmware goes in as a UF2 file over the USB-C: hold BOOTSEL while plugging the cable in
# (or QMK's bootloader key / double tap of reset), the board shows up as a USB drive.  The QMK keyboard definition is
# in ../../firmware/qmk/ (pin table below).
#
# 3.3 V: the RP2040's pins take NO 5 V.  The matrix (from the prototype) ran on 5 V; what changes for it:
#   - the 16 column pull-ups (R169-R184) hang on VCC3V3 instead of VCC5 (OVERRIDE below; on the board their supply
#     track is cut off the 5 V bus and fed from the LDO)
#   - the rows are driven 0 / 3.3 V; the phototransistors' collectors (columns) then stay at or under 3.3 V
#   - the infra-red LEDs keep their 5 V (560 ohm from VCC5 to LED_K, switched by Q1); R185, Q1's base resistor, is 330
#     ohm instead of 1 k so that a 3.3 V output still drives Q1 hard (about 7 mA base current for 0.56 A)
#   - T16's LED hangs between 5 V (560 ohm) and row A0: with A0 high (3.3 V) about 1 mA flows back into that pin, which
#     the chip's own 3.3 V consumption takes; it is never above 3.3 V
# Kept: USB-C (device side, 5.1 k on both CC pins), the ESD array, the series resistors (27 ohm now, the RP2040's
# value), the resettable fuse (750 mA: the 84 infra-red LEDs draw about 0.56 A when all are on).
# Net names are still the ARDUINO pin names (D0..D13, A0..A5, MISO / SCK / MOSI) the prototype used: they name the
# matrix lines, not chip pins any more.  References are in a 200-block (no collision with the Altium parts).
SHEET kbd_mcu

# --- microcontroller: supply pins, the exposed pad is the only ground ---
U201 RP2040 1=VCC3V3 10=VCC3V3 22=VCC3V3 33=VCC3V3 42=VCC3V3 49=VCC3V3 43=VCC3V3 44=VCC3V3 48=VCC3V3 57=GND
# core supply (1.1 V) from the chip's own regulator: VREG_VOUT to both DVDD pins
P U201 45=VDD11 23=VDD11 50=VDD11
# TESTEN to ground (as on every RP2040 board); RUN has an internal pull-up; SWD left open (no debug pads)
P U201 19=GND
NC U201 24 25 26
# crystal, USB, flash
P U201 20=XIN 21=XOUT 46=USB_MCU_DM 47=USB_MCU_DP
P U201 51=QSPI_SD3 52=QSPI_SCLK 53=QSPI_SD0 54=QSPI_SD2 55=QSPI_SD1 56=QSPI_SS
# the matrix lines, the LED enable (D0_RX) and the power-key output (PWRKEY_DRV, see 09_kbd_powerkey.net) on the GPIOs
# that face where each line arrives at the chip (no crossings):
# >>> GPIO table (written by tools/kbd_rp2040_board.py --stage plan) >>>
P U201 2=D0_RX 3=A2 4=SCK 5=MISO 6=SS_RXLED 8=D2_SDA 9=D3_SCL 11=D4 12=D5 13=MOSI 14=D7 15=PWRKEY_DRV 17=D6 27=D8 28=D9 29=D11 30=D12 31=D13 32=A0 34=A1 35=D10 36=A3 37=A4 38=A5
NC U201 7 16 18 39 40 41    # spare GPIO: GPIO5 GPIO13 GPIO15 GPIO27 GPIO28 GPIO29
# GPIO of each line (QMK pin names):
#   D0_RX      GP0
#   A2         GP1
#   SCK        GP2
#   MISO       GP3
#   SS_RXLED   GP4
#   D2_SDA     GP6
#   D3_SCL     GP7
#   D4         GP8
#   D5         GP9
#   MOSI       GP10
#   D7         GP11
#   PWRKEY_DRV GP12
#   D6         GP14
#   D8         GP16
#   D9         GP17
#   D11        GP18
#   D12        GP19
#   D13        GP20
#   A0         GP21
#   A1         GP22
#   D10        GP23
#   A3         GP24
#   A4         GP25
#   A5         GP26
# <<< GPIO table <<<

# --- decoupling: 100 nF at the I/O supply pins, 1 uF on the regulator's input and output, 100 nF at the core pins ---
C203 100nF_0603 VCC3V3 GND
C204 100nF_0603 VCC3V3 GND
C206 100nF_0603 VCC3V3 GND
C207 100nF_0603 VCC3V3 GND
C208 1uF_0603 VCC3V3 GND
C201 1uF_0603 VDD11 GND
C202 100nF_0603 VDD11 GND

# --- 3.3 V: LDO from the USB 5 V (the RP2040 and the flash take about 30 mA, the pull-ups 5 mA) ---
U203 ME6211:ME6211C33M5G 1=VCC5 3=VCC5 2=GND 5=VCC3V3
NC U203 4
C209 4.7uF_0603 VCC5 GND
C205 4.7uF_0603 VCC3V3 GND

# --- 12 MHz crystal (YXC X322512MSB4SI, C9002, 20 pF load): 1 k in XOUT as on the Raspberry Pi Pico, 22 pF each side ---
Y201 XTAL4:12MHz 1=XIN 3=XTAL_O 2=GND 4=GND
R208 1K_0402 XOUT XTAL_O
C212 22pF_0402 XIN GND
C213 22pF_0402 XTAL_O GND

# --- program flash: 2 MB, quad SPI ---
U204 QSPI_FLASH_SO8:GD25Q16ESIGR 1=QSPI_SS 2=QSPI_SD1 3=QSPI_SD2 4=GND 5=QSPI_SD0 6=QSPI_SCLK 7=QSPI_SD3 8=VCC3V3
C211 100nF_0603 VCC3V3 GND

# --- BOOTSEL: hold while plugging in USB -> the RP2040's USB boot ROM (a "RPI-RP2" drive for the UF2 file) ---
R206 1K_0603 QSPI_SS BOOTSEL_N
SW201 TACT:BOOTSEL 1=BOOTSEL_N 2=GND

# --- supply: USB bus power only ---
F201 PTC:750mA VBUS_USB VCC5

# --- USB-C, device side, on the left edge of the board (the free bay beside the codec column), MID-MOUNT in a notch
#     of the edge, pads on the bottom face (2026-11-19: flush with the top, 1.6 mm below the bottom; HCTL
#     HC-TYPE-C-16P-CB1.6 like the numpad / touchpad); the power-key
#     cable J202 sits next to it (09_kbd_powerkey.net) ---
J201 USBC16_MID A1=GND A12=GND B1=GND B12=GND SH=GND A4=VBUS_USB A9=VBUS_USB B4=VBUS_USB B9=VBUS_USB
P J201 A5=KB_CC1 B5=KB_CC2 A6=USB_CON_DP B6=USB_CON_DP A7=USB_CON_DM B7=USB_CON_DM
NC J201 A8 B8
R201 5.1K_0603 KB_CC1 GND
R202 5.1K_0603 KB_CC2 GND
U202 USBLC6-2SC6 1=USB_CON_DP 6=USB_CON_DP 3=USB_CON_DM 4=USB_CON_DM 2=GND 5=VBUS_USB
C210 4.7uF_0603 VBUS_USB GND
# series resistors at the MCU end of the two data lines (27 ohm: the RP2040's USB, as on the Pico)
R203 27R_0603 USB_CON_DP USB_MCU_DP
R204 27R_0603 USB_CON_DM USB_MCU_DM
